1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-0--0c-immerse-in-the-language","kind":"phase-article","route":"/roadmap/phase-0/0c-immerse-in-the-language","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-0/0c-immerse-in-the-language","sourceHash":"92a77447d6f42262a612417b6e296a7ad9a08a98790acbcde81130fae2986247","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:33.092Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"0C — Immerse in the Language","zh":"0C — 沉浸于目标语言"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The core of the Refold method is \"immersion\" — spending time doing things in the target language, usually consuming media. It does <em>not</em> mean living in a country that speaks the language (though that would certainly add to it).","zh":"Refold 方法的核心是“沉浸”——花时间用目标语言做事情，通常是消费媒体内容。这<em>并不</em>意味着必须生活在使用该语言的国家（尽管那当然会有所帮助）。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"It may feel odd and unproductive to spend time watching TV in your target language. Rest assured: it's not a guilty pleasure. <strong>It is where language acquisition happens.</strong> Even when something is mostly incomprehensible, your brain is hard at work looking for patterns, testing hypotheses, and building your internal language machine.","zh":"花时间用目标语言看电视，可能会让你觉得奇怪又没效率。请放心：这不是一种需要内疚的享受。<strong>语言习得正是在这里发生的。</strong>即使内容大多难以理解，你的大脑也在努力寻找模式、检验假设，并构建自己的内部语言系统。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"When you first start immersing, your brain will discard most of the information because it doesn't think it's important. You'll probably find it hard to focus for more than a few minutes at a time. But focusing intently on target language content signals to the brain that it needs to start paying attention and learning.","zh":"刚开始沉浸时，你的大脑会丢弃大部分信息，因为它认为那些信息并不重要。你可能会发现，自己一次很难集中注意力超过几分钟。但专注地关注目标语言内容，会向大脑发出信号：它需要开始留意并学习。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This \"focus\" is what we call active immersion — time spent actively <em>trying</em> to understand, even if you don't.","zh":"我们把这种“专注”称为主动沉浸——花时间主动<em>尝试</em>理解，即使你并没有真正理解。"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Active immersion is the most important activity in language learning.</strong>","zh":"<strong>主动沉浸是语言学习中最重要的活动。</strong>"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"If you aren't paying attention or focused on the language, you're not learning. Immersion isn't magic (even though it may feel like it sometimes). You gotta put in hard work if you want it to work.","zh":"如果你没有注意语言，或没有专注于语言，你就没有在学习。沉浸并不是魔法（尽管有时可能会让你产生这种感觉）。如果你希望它发挥作用，就必须付出艰苦的努力。"}},{"id":"types-of-immersion","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Types of Immersion","zh":"沉浸的类型"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Throughout the roadmap, you'll encounter different types of immersion. They're introduced at the points where they become most useful, but here's a brief overview:","zh":"在整个路线图中，你会遇到不同类型的沉浸。它们会在最有用的阶段被介绍；下面先做一个简要概览："}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Interactive immersion</strong> is when you use tools and techniques to understand more than you otherwise would — pausing, looking things up, replaying audio. This is your primary immersion activity in the early phases. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/interactive-reading-with-audio\">Interactive Reading with Audio</a>","zh":"<strong>互动式沉浸</strong>是指使用工具和技巧，让自己比原本能理解更多内容——暂停、查找资料、重放音频等。这是早期阶段的主要沉浸活动。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/interactive-reading-with-audio\">带音频的互动式阅读</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Freeflow immersion</strong> is closer to what you do in your native language — you focus on the content and let understanding happen naturally, without stopping to look things up. This becomes important once you have enough base knowledge. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/freeflow-immersion\">Freeflow Immersion</a>","zh":"<strong>自由流沉浸</strong>更接近你使用母语时的方式——专注于内容，让理解自然发生，不停下来查找资料。掌握足够的基础知识后，这种方式会变得非常重要。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/freeflow-immersion\">自由流沉浸</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Both types are critical. The balance between them shifts as you progress. The line between isn't as clear-cut as I make it seem. They really exist on a spectrum from more interactive to more freeflow. But the distinction is still valuable.","zh":"这两种类型都至关重要。随着你不断进步，它们之间的平衡会发生变化。两者之间的界线并不像我描述的那么清晰。实际上，它们存在于一个从更互动到更自由流的连续光谱上。但区分它们仍然很有价值。"}},{"id":"finding-content","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Finding Content","zh":"寻找内容"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"In order to immerse, you need <em>things</em> (TV, movies, books, podcasts, articles, etc.) in your target language. Finding the right content matters a lot, and we give specific recommendations in each phase. But the general principle is simple: <strong>you must enjoy what you're immersing in.</strong> <a href=\"/roadmap/phase-0/0d-enjoy-the-process\">0D — Enjoy the Process</a>","zh":"为了进行沉浸，你需要目标语言中的<em>内容</em>（电视节目、电影、书籍、播客、文章等）。找到合适的内容非常重要，我们会在每个阶段给出具体建议。但总原则很简单：<strong>你必须喜欢自己用来沉浸的内容。</strong><a href=\"/roadmap/phase-0/0d-enjoy-the-process\">0D — 享受过程</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Since the method is built so heavily on content immersion, enjoying your materials isn't optional — it's a core requirement for success. Different kinds of materials will be more or less useful at different stages, but enjoyment is always the top priority.","zh":"由于该方法高度依赖内容沉浸，享受你的学习材料并不是可有可无的——这是成功的核心要求。不同阶段适合的材料类型会有所不同，但享受内容始终是第一优先级。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Start in places where you already consume content. If you watch YouTube, that's a great source. If you like reading books, they can be part of your learning. The more your immersion looks like something you'd do for fun, the more likely you are to stick with it.","zh":"从你平时已经会消费内容的地方开始。如果你看 YouTube，那里就是很好的内容来源。如果你喜欢读书，书籍也可以成为学习的一部分。沉浸越像你为了娱乐而做的事情，你就越有可能坚持下去。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与论证"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The idea that language acquisition happens through exposure to understandable messages — comprehensible input — was formalized by Krashen (1982) and has been extensively supported and refined over the following decades. The core claim that your brain needs to understand what it's hearing in order to acquire the language is well-established, even among researchers who disagree with Krashen on other points.","zh":"语言习得通过接触可理解的信息——即可理解输入——发生；这一观点由 Krashen（1982）系统提出，并在随后几十年中得到广泛支持和完善。你的大脑需要理解所听到的内容，才能习得语言；这一核心主张已经得到充分确立，即使有些研究者在其他方面并不认同 Krashen。"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The distinction between interactive and freeflow immersion maps onto concepts from the broader SLA literature. Interactive immersion aligns with what researchers call \"form-focused\" or \"meaning-focused\" activity with attention to language, while freeflow immersion is closer to extensive reading/listening. Research on extensive reading (Day &amp; Bamford, 1998) and incidental vocabulary acquisition (Webb, 2020) supports the value of large volumes of meaning-focused input. Meanwhile, studies on reading-while-listening (Webb &amp; Chang, 2015) show that combining text with audio is more effective for vocabulary acquisition than either channel alone — which is why we recommend it so heavily in the early phases.","zh":"互动式沉浸和自由流动式沉浸的区别，与更广泛的第二语言习得（SLA）研究中的一些概念相对应。互动式沉浸类似于研究者所说的“聚焦形式”或“聚焦意义”的活动，同时关注语言本身；而自由流动式沉浸则更接近广泛阅读/听力。关于广泛阅读（Day &amp; Bamford，1998）和偶然词汇习得（Webb，2020）的研究支持大量以意义为中心的输入所具有的价值。同时，关于边读边听（Webb &amp; Chang，2015）的研究表明，将文本与音频结合起来，比单独使用任何一种渠道都更有利于词汇习得——这也是我们在早期阶段大力推荐这种方式的原因。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
